Contract review that comes back as a real Word redline — not a summary of one.
 
Attach your contract, name your playbook, and get your own .docx back with native tracked changes and margin comments. The mark ups are
against your organization's positions exactly as an attorney or contracting professional would do it in Word's Review pane. Accept or reject
each change one at a time, the way you already work.

Built by an attorney and contracts professional for the people who do this work every day: attorneys, small business owners, procurement and
purchasing offices, contract managers, and research administrators.

What it does:
 
- Produces real tracked changes. Every edit is a genuine Word revision that is accept/reject-able, attributed, reversible. Not colored text
pretending to be a redline. Not a chat window full of advice you then implement by hand.
- Returns your document, not a retyped copy. It edits your file in place: formatting, numbering, tables, and every word it isn't deliberately
changing pass through untouched. A built-in validation gate mechanically proves it, and rejecting every tracked change reproduces your original
exactly. Inserted text matches your contract's own font and heading style.
- Marks up the way a lawyer or contracting professional does. It amends the section that already covers a topic instead of bolting a contradictory
clause onto the end. Liability caps land inside your liability section as a proviso. It strikes the fewest words necessary rather than gutting
whole paragraphs. New clauses go beside the terms they relate to.
- Never drops anything silently. Any change it can't apply cleanly becomes an "ACTION NEEDED" margin comment containing the full suggested
language, so you can place it yourself. You always see everything it wanted to do.
- Applies your rules, not generic ones. Every legal position comes from a playbook. Six starting playbooks are included: revenue services,
sponsored research, expense services (general, SaaS, and software), and mutual NDAs. 

What it does not do:
 
- It is not a lawyer, and its output is not legal advice. Every redline is AI-generated and must be reviewed by a qualified attorney or contracting
professional before any contract is finalized or executed. It does not create an attorney-client relationship.
- The playbooks are a starting point, not your positions. The included playbooks reflect common contracting postures, and they are written to be
edited by your organization. Before relying on them, coordinate with your own legal counsel and contracting professionals to adapt each playbook
to your organization's requirements, risk tolerance, and governing law. A playbook you haven't reviewed is a playbook you haven't approved.
- It does not decide your legal positions. It enforces the ones written in the playbook. If your position isn't in the playbook, the tool won't
invent it.
- It may not renumber your sections. Where a contract uses typed (non-automatic) numbering, new sections are inserted unnumbered and flagged for
you because a wrong number silently breaks every cross-reference in the document.
- It will not catch everything. It reviews against your playbook's rules. A human still must read the contract.
- It is not a hosted service. It runs inside your own AI assistant subscription. 

What you'll need:
 
- A subscription to a code-capable AI assistant (Claude, ChatGPT, and similar tools) with code execution and file creation enabled. You are
STRONGLY ENCOURAGED to obtain an ENTERPRISE-licensed tool with engineered controls or a contractual commitment to not use your contracts to train
your provider’s models.
- Word (.docx) contracts work best; PDFs are supported. Setup takes about ten minutes and is covered step by step in the included instructions.

What's included:

- six contracting playbooks
- the redline engine
- the assistant instructions and process file
- an optional accelerator for Claude using Claude Skills
- setup-and-use documentation


